WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:01.780
- A lot of people are throwing around

00:00:01.780 --> 00:00:03.250
the word sedition recently,

00:00:03.250 --> 00:00:06.080
and unfortunately not for no good reason.

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:08.440
But what is sedition itself?

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:10.740
Well, originally as used
in the United States,

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:12.920
sedition meant opposing or resisting

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:14.660
any law of the United States

00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:17.200
or writing anything false,
malicious, or scandalous

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:19.140
about the president or Congress.

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:21.320
But because of the First
Amendment, that doesn't fly.

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:24.730
So now the law is codified at 18 USC 2384,

00:00:24.730 --> 00:00:26.190
which makes it a crime
for two or more people

00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:28.460
within the United States
to conspire to overthrow

00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:31.110
or destroy by force the
government of the United States

00:00:31.110 --> 00:00:32.260
or to levy war against them,

00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:33.700
to oppose by force the authority

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:34.660
of the United States government,

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:36.420
or to take, seize, or possess by force

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:37.860
any property of the United States.

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:39.100
And the next statute makes it a crime

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:40.960
to advocate for the violent overthrow

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:42.160
of the United States government.

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:44.770
Is what we saw on January
6th actually sedition?

00:00:44.770 --> 00:00:46.600
Well, I'm gonna do a
full video to find out,

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:48.153
so I'll see you in court.

